EMK <noneofyourbusiness@columbus.rr.com> wrote:
> 1.  Are there "safe" methods of hoarding stuff?  Are certain locations
> (e.g.  rooms only accessible by opening doors) more secure than others?
> Are there particularly "unsafe" paces to put things (e.g. areas crawling
> with humanoids rather than less intelligent monsters)?  Other than being
> looted by NPCs, is my pile of goodies in any danger of disappearing or
> degrading over time?

Your stashed stuff won't disappear or degrade. Chests, boxes, sacks, and
bags all make equally good stash containers (there's no significant
advantage to one over the other, nor any advantage to locking containers
that can be locked). Monsters won't loot the contents of a container, but
some monsters (e.g. nymphs) can pick the whole container up and move it,
and gel. cubes can engulf the container (kill it to get your stuff back).
If you want to keep creatures from meddling, engrave or burn the E-word
underneath the container (keeps most monsters away), and drop a cursed
item on top of the container (keeps pets away).

You can also use a "closet" (one-square room with a door) as a stash,
but if a monster spawns in there (or opens the door), then they can take
items out of your stash piecemeal.

There aren't really any places that are more/less secure to put the stash.
If you're playing Wizard, the lowest level of Sokoban makes a nice place,
because the Eye lets you branchport right to that level. Monsters can't
take action when you're away from a level, so you will always be on the
stash level at times when you would need to keep an eye on it.

> 2.  I've read that it's a good idea to put potions and fragile objects
> in containers.  So far I've only come across chests and boxes (the
> former don't let me travel diagonally around boulders when I carry them);

Bags and sacks also qualify, and those are what you would carry fragiles
around in. You don't want to lug a box or chest around (too heavy). A bag
of holding is best. But yes, best practice is to keep potions and scrolls
etc. out of main inventory, so they don't get burned, shattered, etc.

> 3.  Is there any way to check on the status of my pet?  I'd like to see
> how my little buddy is progressing.
> 
> 4.  Is there any way to get a sense of how much damage I am dealing or
> how injured my foe is?

The best answer for both of these is a stethoscope. You can #chat to
some pets and the sound they make will give you a (very rough) idea of
their status, but stethoscope reveals all. In combat, you get one free
use of the stethoscope against a monster in melee range (doesn't take a
turn).

Another alternative is a wand of probing, but of course that will run
out of charges eventually.

